[38;2;124;124;124mProblem: If $U$ is a Lie group with a closed subgroup $K$ such that both $U$ and[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$U/K$ are simply-connected, is $K$ necessarily connected?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this. We have a fibration:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$K \to U \to U/K$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a fiber bundle with fiber $K$, total space $U$, base $U/K$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe long exact sequence of homotopy groups for this fibration:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\cdots \to \pi_1(K) \to \pi_1(U) \to \pi_1(U/K) \to \pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U) \to [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\pi_0(U/K) \to 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $U$ is simply-connected: $\pi_1(U) = 0$ and $\pi_0(U) = 0$ (simply-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconnected implies connected).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSince $U/K$ is simply-connected: $\pi_1(U/K) = 0$ and $\pi_0(U/K) = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the sequence becomes:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\cdots \to \pi_1(K) \to 0 \to 0 \to \pi_0(K) \to 0 \to 0 \to 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFrom the exactness at $\pi_0(K)$: the map $\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)$ has kernel = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mimage of $\pi_1(U/K) \to \pi_0(K)$. Since $\pi_1(U/K) = 0$, the image is 0, so [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe kernel of $\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)$ is 0. But $\pi_0(U) = 0$, so the map $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\pi_0(K) \to 0$ has kernel = all of $\pi_0(K)$. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me be more careful. The connecting map is $\pi_1(U/K) \to \pi_0(K)$. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSince $\pi_1(U/K) = 0$, the image of this map is trivial. By exactness at $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\pi_0(K)$: $\ker(\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)) = \text{im}(\pi_1(U/K) \to \pi_0(K)) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m0$. So $\ker(\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)) = 0$, meaning $\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minjective. But $\pi_0(U) = 0$ (since $U$ is simply-connected, hence connected). [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\pi_0(K) = 0$, meaning $K$ is connected.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, that seems straightforward. Let me double-check the long exact sequence.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a fibration $F \to E \to B$, the long exact sequence is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\cdots \to \pi_n(F) \to \pi_n(E) \to \pi_n(B) \to \pi_{n-1}(F) \to \cdots \to [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\pi_0(F) \to \pi_0(E) \to \pi_0(B)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHere $F = K$, $E = U$, $B = U/K$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe relevant part:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\pi_1(U) \to \pi_1(U/K) \to \pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U) \to \pi_0(U/K)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWith $\pi_1(U) = 0$, $\pi_1(U/K) = 0$, $\pi_0(U) = 0$, $\pi_0(U/K) = 0$:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$$0 \to 0 \to \pi_0(K) \to 0 \to 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy exactness at $\pi_0(K)$: $\ker(\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)) = \text{im}(\pi_1(U/K) [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\to \pi_0(K)) = \text{im}(0 \to \pi_0(K)) = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\pi_0(K) \to \pi_0(U)$ is injective. Since $\pi_0(U) = 0$, we get $\pi_0(K) [0m[38;2;124;124;124m= 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $K$ is connected. The answer is yes.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait—I need to be careful about whether the quotient map $U \to U/K$ is a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfibration. For a Lie group $U$ with closed subgroup $K$, the quotient map $U \to[0m[38;2;124;124;124mU/K$ is a principal $K$-bundle, which is indeed a fiber bundle, and fiber [0m
